# ★ THE CHECK WAS THERE. IT RAN AFTER THE WRITE.
#
# The defective version copied the attribute name, then '=', then the value,
# then ',', decrementing a signed counter as it went, and only then asked:
#
#     if (buff_len < 0) { ... return false; }
#
# By the time that is false, the bytes are already in the buffer. The published
# fix deletes it and puts a check in FRONT of the copy that accounts for
# everything the copy will write — the name, the '=', the value, the ',' and
# the terminator:
#
#     if (buff_len < (int32_t)(cur->name_len + 1 + obj_len + 1 + 1))
#
# It also moves the ASN.1 tag fetch above the copy, because until the tag has
# been read obj_len is not yet the length of the thing about to be written.
#
# negative/test_oversized_field.c's defect 7 is that shape: the same refusal,
# after the same copy. It is the reason that file returns a distinct status for
# "refused, and wrote anyway", and the reason every destination in it sits in
# front of a guard region that the test reads by hand.
#
# Two things the advisory says that a version number does not
# -----------------------------------------------------------
# NO CVE WAS ISSUED, and the advisory states why in its own words: "Though a
# CVE severity score is provided, due to the unlikely chance of implementation
# in a production device, no CVE has been issued." It scores 6.9 all the same.
#
# THE ATTACK SURFACE IS BACK-END SPECIFIC. The defect is in cryptlib_mbedtls.
# The same protocol library under the OpenSSL back end does not reach it — and
# post-quantum support exists only under OpenSSL, so "should this device move
# to ML-DSA" is not only an algorithm question: it is a question about which
# crypto back end, and therefore which set of defects, the device inherits.
#
# NOT IN GITHUB'S GLOBAL ADVISORY DATABASE; https://github.com/advisories/
# GHSA-j54w-759w-xj3m returns 404. url= above is the one that resolves.
#
# Not vendored, for third_party/dmtf-2026-0002.pin's reason, word for word.
